@@ -129,3 +129,9 @@ A hand review of the sign-in and campaign access code (routers/auth.py, routers/
 - Gear slots (rulebook check 2026-10-07): "PCs have the capacity for three pieces of gear... Gear slots only reset once an assignment is complete" (p. 52). A player could unmark gear at any time, freeing a used slot, and ending the assignment never cleared it; the gear dialog also dropped any marked item it did not list. A player's update_gear that unmarks an item is now refused (409), the Lightkeeper may still correct the list, gm_end_assignment clears every member's gear, and the dialog keeps every marked item, locked. Tests: test_update_gear, test_the_gm_may_correct_marked_gear, test_gm_end_assignment.
 - ability_uses (pre-deploy review 2026-10-07): get_char_dict sent a string ability_uses through unparsed (the quirk above), and the desk's gear slots and once-per-assignment Use buttons read it as an object. On a database whose column is TEXT, a Geared Up ally saw three slots and every Use button looked ready. It is now parsed like gear and scars, and anything unreadable is sent as {}. Tests: test_char_dict_parses_string_ability_uses, test_roll_with_string_ability_uses_works.
 - Row locks on a refused ability (pre-deploy review 2026-10-07): use_ability locked the campaign circle for Volunteer Duty and the target for Ritual, and its refusals (resources not open, already spent, no burned resistance) returned with the lock held until that socket's next message. Any other write to the row, such as the GM opening resources or the ally's own roll, waited 5 seconds for it, stalled every socket meanwhile, and failed. The refusal now rolls back first, and the socket loop ends each message's transaction after its handler (vtt/ws/endpoint.py). Test: test_a_refused_use_ability_leaves_no_row_locked.
+
+## Dice, connection and abilities (playtest, 2026-10-09)
+
+Found by the AI playtest of 2026-10-08 and verified on a fresh copy (its report's keys in brackets). Each fix has its own commit and test.
+
+- A gilded roll waiting for its die (gilded-reload-free-reroll): the server held the dice but never offered them again, so a desk reloaded during the choice forgot the roll, nothing had logged it, and the next roll replaced it: a free reroll, with the drive the first one spent lost. A socket that opens on the character now gets the held roll's roll_result again. Test: test_a_desk_that_opens_again_is_offered_the_held_gilded_roll.
